"a": "<p><b>10-K:</b> the annual report every US public company must file with the SEC. It is comprehensive — a full overview of the business, management’s commentary on performance, risk factors, disclosures on any accounting policy changes and, most importantly, the three core financial statements with supporting data.</p><p><b>10-Q:</b> the quarterly report filed with the SEC. It is much shorter and less detailed than the 10-K, centred on the quarter’s financials with only brief MD&amp;A and supplementary disclosures.</p><p><b>Other differences:</b> the 10-K must be audited by an independent accounting firm, whereas the 10-Q is only reviewed by CPAs and stays unaudited. Timing also differs — a 10-K is due roughly 60–90 days after fiscal year-end, a 10-Q roughly 40–45 days after quarter-end.</p>",
