"a": "<p>The income statement shows a company’s accrual-based profitability over a period, which lets you analyse its historical growth and operating performance. The main lines, top to bottom:</p><table><tr><th>Line item</th><th>What it is</th></tr><tr><td>Net revenue (sales)</td><td>The “top line” — total value of goods sold and services delivered in the period.</td></tr><tr><td>Less: COGS</td><td>Costs directly tied to producing that revenue, e.g. materials and direct labour.</td></tr><tr><td>Gross profit</td><td>Revenue − COGS</td></tr><tr><td>Less: SG&amp;A</td><td>Operating costs not directly tied to the product or service sold — payroll, overhead, advertising, marketing.</td></tr><tr><td>Less: R&amp;D</td><td>Spending to develop new products or processes and improve the existing offering.</td></tr><tr><td>EBITDA</td><td>Gross profit − SG&amp;A − R&amp;D. Earnings Before Interest, Taxes, Depreciation &amp; Amortization.</td></tr><tr><td>Less: D&amp;A</td><td>Non-cash expense estimating the yearly decline in value of fixed and intangible assets.</td></tr><tr><td>Operating income (EBIT)</td><td>EBITDA − D&amp;A. Earnings Before Interest and Taxes.</td></tr><tr><td>Less: interest expense, net</td><td>Interest on debt, net of interest income earned on investments.</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-tax income (EBT)</td><td>EBIT − net interest expense</td></tr><tr><td>Less: tax expense</td><td>The tax recorded for book (GAAP) purposes.</td></tr><tr><td>Net income</td><td>EBT − taxes: the “bottom line.”</td></tr></table><p class=\"muted\">The Red Book illustrates this with Apple’s 2016–2019 income statement (p. 15).</p>",
