"id": "accounting-questions-9",
"q": "Walk me through the cash flow statement.",
"a": "<p>A cash flow statement can be prepared using the direct or the indirect method. The indirect method is far more common, and it splits the statement into three sections:</p><p><b>Cash from operations:</b> starts from net income, adds back non-cash expenses such as D&amp;A and stock-based compensation, then adjusts for changes in working capital.</p><p><b>Cash from investing:</b> capital expenditures — usually the biggest outflow — followed by any acquisitions or divestitures.</p><p><b>Cash from financing:</b> the net cash effect of raising debt or equity, less cash spent on share buybacks and debt repayments. Dividends paid to shareholders are also an outflow here.</p><p>The three sections add up to the net change in cash for the period. Add that to the beginning cash balance and you get the ending cash balance.</p>",
