"id": "accounting-questions-13",
"q": "If you had to pick between either the income statement or cash flow statement to analyze a company, which would you pick?",
"a": "<p>Usually the <b>cash flow statement</b>. It shows a company’s real liquidity and isn’t exposed to the judgement calls that accrual accounting allows. Whether you’re lending to or investing in the business, what matters first is its ability to generate enough free cash flow to reinvest in operations and service its debt — “cash is king.”</p><p>The answer can flip for an <b>unprofitable</b> company. If net income, cash from operations and free cash flow are all negative, the CFS is less useful for valuation, and you’d lean on the income statement to value the business on a revenue multiple instead.</p>",
