"q": "Why is the income statement insufficient to assess the liquidity of a company?",
"a": "<p>The income statement can give a misleading picture of a company’s liquidity and solvency.</p><p>For example, a company can report positive net income period after period while failing to collect the cash from its credit sales. That collection problem would never show up on the income statement.</p><p>Accrual accounting also leaves room for management discretion. That “wiggle room” in reporting choices raises the risk of earnings management and of results that misrepresent how the business is really performing — so the IS is less reliable on its own.</p><p>The fix is the cash flow statement, which reconciles net income to the actual cash inflows and outflows, showing the true cash effect of operating, investing and financing activity in the period.</p>",
